[h1]R.A.T.S. Keel[/h1]
[i]R.A.T.S. – Reconnaissance, Acoustics, Terrain & Signals[/i]
[b]Bugs, ideas, questions:[/b] [url=https://hub.sewerlabs.dev/s/keel]R.A.T.S. feedback board[/url]

[h2]A replacement for Anchor Chain's loader[/h2]
Sea Power's code mods need a mod loader to start them. For most players that has been Anchor Chain. [b]Keel does the same job and takes its place:[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Your Anchor Chain mods keep working.[/b] Nothing to change in them, no new versions needed
[*][b]You no longer need the Anchor Chain item.[/b] Keel brings Anchor Chain's official library file (1.1.0, MIT, byte for byte) for the mods built against it
[*][b]Plain BepInEx plugins run too[/b]
[*][b]On top of that, Keel looks before it loads:[/b] it checks every mod against your game version, starts each one on its own, and tells you what happened and why
[/list]
[b]One bad mod can't ruin your game anymore.[/b]

[h2]Install in one click[/h2]
Subscribe, then run [b]KeelSetup.exe[/b] from Keel's Workshop folder (Steam: right-click Sea Power, Manage, Browse local files, then go up to [i]steamapps\workshop\content\1286220\3835527459[/i]). It finds the game, adds BepInEx if needed, installs Keel's starter file and switches Anchor Chain's (or Alpine's) preloader off, without deleting anything. [b]Restore[/b] puts it all back. Then switch R.A.T.S. Keel on in the game's Mods menu and restart.
Windows may say "unknown publisher" because the file is not signed yet: More info, Run anyway.

[b]By hand, switching from Anchor Chain:[/b]
[olist]
[*]Subscribe and switch R.A.T.S. Keel on in the game's Mods menu
[*]Copy [b]SeaPowerKeel.Stub.dll[/b] from [i]copy-to-BepInEx-plugins[/i] in Keel's Workshop folder into [i]Sea Power\BepInEx\plugins[/i] (once; it never needs replacing, updates come through the Workshop)
[*]Start the game, then restart it once. Ctrl+Shift+K shows Keel's report
[/olist]
[b]Keel is the loader while it is on.[/b] Anchor Chain's or Alpine's preloader can stay where it is: Keel keeps it from starting, so every mod starts once, by Keel. Their files are not touched; switch Keel off in the Mods menu and they start again. On the very first start Keel sets this up and leaves their mods to them once; restart and Keel runs everything.

[h2]Keeps your game running[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Wrong-version mods are caught before they load.[/b] Keel checks everything a mod uses from the game against the version you run. A DLL that would stop every mission from loading is left out, and the report says why
[*][b]One failure doesn't take the others down.[/b] Every mod starts on its own; one that fails is marked and the next one starts. No endless loading loop, no frozen game
[*][b]Bad patches are stopped before they start.[/b] A patch that does not fit the game method would corrupt memory, so Keel leaves it out
[*][b]No more error-popup floods.[/b] After a game update, an outdated mod can bury your screen in error popups every frame. Keel names the mod and takes its broken patch off, so you keep playing
[*][b]A mod that crashed the game stays out[/b] until it is updated, or until you click Let held mods run
[*][b]Right build for your game.[/b] Mods that ship one DLL for the flat map and one for the 3D globe say which is which in a small keel.ini, and Keel starts only the one that fits
[*][b]Only the mods you switched on.[/b] Keel follows the game's Mods menu; switched-off mods are not even opened
[/list]

[h2]Tells you what happened[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]A crash window that names the mod.[/b] The moment the game dies, a window says which mod it was when Keel can tell: the one starting, the one whose code was running, or the pack whose file was loading. Tick a box to keep it off and restart
[*][b]Bug reports in one click.[/b] Save logs puts every log into one zip on your Desktop. Send report sends them to the R.A.T.S. developer with your Windows user name removed, only when you click Send
[*][b]The report.[/b] Ctrl+Shift+K shows every mod: running, running with errors, not started and why (missing dependency, conflict, wrong game build). Also in BepInEx\Keel-report.txt
[*][b]A loading window.[/b] While the game starts, a small window shows each mod as it starts and how long it takes, then a summary card on the main menu
[*][b]Logs with times.[/b] BepInEx\Keel-log.txt has every line of the game and all mods with the time and the mod each error came from; the last sessions are kept
[*][b]A readable version question.[/b] When the game asks about mods made for another version, Keel lists short names, code mods apart from data mods, with the version each was made for
[/list]

[h2]Handy extras[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Restart from the Mods menu.[/b] The game's reload dialog gets a Restart button: saves your list, closes the game and starts it again
[*][b]Reload follows your changes.[/b] After the game's Reload, Keel starts code mods you switched on and takes switched-off ones out as far as the running game allows; Restart is the clean way
[*][b]A faster start.[/b] In our test, eight switched-on mods were running 37 s after launch; the loader used before, which also loaded the switched-off ones, needed 116 s
[/list]

[h2]What it changes[/h2]
Keel changes nothing in the game, in missions or in saves: it decides which code mods start, in which order, and tells you about it. What a mod does is up to that mod. One exception, only if you leave the box ticked in the crash window: Keel switches that pack off in the Mods menu.
Keel cannot stop a crash inside the game's engine or your graphics driver; it tells you what it knows and keeps the same mod from doing it again. KeelWatcher.exe is the small program that shows the crash window, because a crashed game cannot. Logs saved with Save logs contain file paths, so also your Windows user name.

[h2]Game versions[/h2]
One Keel for both game versions; it uses nothing that differs between them. Run in the game on 0.9.0 (build 261111): start-up with 17 mods, Anchor Chain mods included, with no Anchor Chain on the PC, plus Reload, Restart and Send report. Run on 0.8.7 (flat map) with an earlier version, next to Anchor Chain and on its own; on 0.8.7 keep Anchor Chain subscribed, that is the tested way there. The crash window and patch checks were run with test mods that break on purpose.

[h2]Settings[/h2]
BepInEx\config\dev.sewerlabs.seapowerkeel.cfg: how strict the version check is, plugins to leave off, Anchor Chain mods on or off, broken-patch removal, holding out crashed mods, the report key, the log, the loading window.

[h2]For mod makers[/h2]
[b]Your mod loads as built.[/b] Nothing to recompile, nothing to add, no need to reference Keel.
[list]
[*][b]List R.A.T.S. Keel as your required item.[/b] Keel brings Anchor Chain's library, so your players need no Anchor Chain item and none of its manual install steps: subscribe, run KeelSetup once, play
[*][b]Anchor Chain mods run as they are:[/b] [ACPlugin], IAnchorChainMod, ACDependency, ACIncompatibility, ACConfig, AnchorChainLoader.FindFile
[*][b]Plain BepInEx plugins run too:[/b] [BepInPlugin], BepInDependency, BepInIncompatibility
[*][b]Bug reports that say what broke.[/b] When a game update breaks your mod, Keel's report names the missing method, field or type and the Harmony patch whose target is gone. Save logs gives players one zip with every log to attach to their report
[*][b]One mod, both game builds.[/b] Ship a flat-map and a 3D-globe DLL and a small keel.ini, and Keel starts the one that fits
[/list]
Details: docs/for-mod-authors.md in the source.

[h2]Credits[/h2]
Written from scratch. Anchor Chain by the SeaPower-Modders is the loader Sea Power code mods grew up on; Keel reads Anchor Chain mods through their public attributes and ships its library unchanged.

Source (MIT): https://github.com/struckl/SeaPowerKeel
The name, logo, pictures and texts are not part of the MIT license: please do not re-upload Keel or a copy of it.

[i]R.A.T.S. – Ratte's Assorted Tactical Systems[/i]
